course: dictionary
lesson: 7648
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Projection is one of them, so the sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer wants. Ask: what is this word doing in this sentence? «A projection is a chosen way of flattening, and each one gives something up: true shapes, true sizes, true distances or true directions.» Here the clue is flattening, so this projection is about drawing a round Earth on a flat map.
explain: The same word can mean something quite different in another sentence. In a science lesson, a projector makes a projection on the wall, which is an image. In a maths lesson, a sales projection is a guess about future numbers, based on what is happening now. Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
explain: «No projection is wrong and none is perfect; the clever question is always what this map was made for.» The word perfect is a clue. A projection cannot be perfect, so it always gives something up. You do not have to know every detail to see the point: each map was made for a purpose.
fact: Projection is a noun, and it can also be plural: projections.
fact: A projection can be an image shown on a screen or wall by a projector.
fact: A projection can be a guess about the future, based on numbers now.
fact: A projection can be a part that sticks out from something.
fact: In maps, a projection is a way of drawing the round Earth on a flat map, and every flat map stretches some places, such as Greenland on many world maps.
fact: In psychology, projection means blaming others for feelings you have yourself.
exercise: «A projection is a chosen way of flattening, and each one gives something up.» What does projection mean in this sentence? | *A way of drawing the round Earth on a flat map | A part that sticks out from a box | A picture shown on a wall by a projector | A guess about next year's sales
exercise: The shop's sales projection says 500 cups will sell next month. What is a projection here? | *A guess about the future, based on numbers now | Blaming others for your feelings | An image on a screen | A part that sticks out
exercise: Ben shouts at his sister for being messy, but he is the one who is messy and cross. | *Projection, blaming others for your own feelings | Projection, a picture shown on a wall | Projection, a part that sticks out | Projection, a guess about the future
test: «Equal-area projections show every country at its true size compared with others, but they bend shapes.» What does the sentence suggest a projection gives up? | *Its true sizes compared with others | Its colours | Its names | Its true shapes
test: In class the teacher uses a projector to make a projection on the board. What is the projection? | *An image shown on the board | A part of the board that sticks out | A map drawn on paper | A guess about future numbers
exam: «No projection is wrong and none is perfect; the clever question is always what this map was made for.» Why is it clever to ask what a map was made for? | Because projections are only used in psychology | Because all maps are made in the same way | Because maps are always wrong and should be thrown away | *Because every projection gives something up, so the purpose tells you which one to trust
